    "https://hr.wisc.edu/postdoc-and-research-intern-vacancies/": {
      "title": "Postdoc, Postgrad, and Research Intern Vacancies",
      "description": "Postdoc, Postgrad, and Research Intern Vacancies | Human Resources | UW–Madison Skip to main content U niversity of W isconsin –Madison UW Crest Human Resources Search Menu open menu close Home Pay Benefits Policies Contact HR Jobs at UW Home Postdoc, Postgrad, and Research Intern Vacancies Postdoc, Postgrad, and Research Intern Vacancies This listing does not include all postdoctoral or research intern vacancies at UW–Madison. These positions are not required to be posted here, but this website is offered as an aid to both departments and applicants. Postdoc Positions Postdoc appointments are held by individuals who are continuing training after receipt of the doctorate. There are two types of postdoc appointments at UW–Madison: Service appointments with the title Research Associate, and Non-Service appointments with the title Postdoctoral Fellow or Postdoctoral Trainee. The distinction between service and non-service appointments is outlined in the Non-Service Appointments policy . Research Intern Positions A Research Intern is an employee who typically is continuing training after receipt of a bachelor’s or master’s degree, rather than a doctorate. Postgraduate Trainee Positions A Postgraduate Trainee is a graduate of an accredited medical school (or other appropriate health science professional school) who is in postgraduate training administered through one of the following schools: School of Medicine and Public Health, School of Nursing, School of Pharmacy, or School of Veterinary Medicine. Post a vacancy The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report contains current campus safety and disciplinary policies, crime statistics for the previous 3 calendar years, and on-campus student housing fire safety policies and fire statistics for the previous 3 calendar years. UW–Madison will provide a paper copy upon request; please contact the University of Wisconsin Police Department . The Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986 requires the University to verify the identity and work authorization of the successful applicant. Any offer of employment is contingent upon verification. The University of Wisconsin is an equal opportunity and affirmative action employer. Title Research Area Department Mentor Deadline Position Requirements Position Duties Application Instructions Website Salary Percent Time Start Date Contact Name Contact Email Contact Phone Status Posted id Postdoctoral Trainee (Postdoc) child welfare Institute for Research on Poverty Lawrence Berger 01/01/2027 Required Qualifications: 1. Research experience or interest focusing on social welfare policy, economic resources, family functioning, and child wellbeing. 2. At least two years of experience with data management and statistical data analysis. Quantitative statistical analysis skills in econometric or related field utilizing statistical analysis packages (such as SPSS, SAS, R, Python or STATA, with a preference for STATA). Familiarity with causal inference for methods for observational studies, such as Difference-in-Differences, regression discontinuity, and instrumental variables methodologies. Desired Qualifications: 1. Experience utilizing and managing large databases. 2. Ability to work within a large research team collaborating with faculty, data scientists, and research assistants. Education: PhD required – economics, public policy, demography, social work, sociology, or a related discipline. We are seeing a postdoctoral scholar whose work focuses on social welfare policy, economic resources, family functioning, and child wellbeing. The postdoc will primarily collaborate with Lawrence (Lonnie) Berger, Maria Cancian (Georgetown University), Jessica Pac, and other scholars on quantitative research in three primary areas: (1) understanding the economic, social, and health impacts of involvement with public programs, including but not limited to the child welfare program, the child support enforcement program, and public assistance programs, on families and children; (2) understanding the causes and consequences of child maltreatment and child protective services involvement, as well as the influence of foster care, on subsequent life trajectories; and (3) estimating the impact of social policies on economic, social, and health outcomes on children and families more generally. In consultation with faculty researchers, the post-doctoral researcher will conduct independent analyses as part of a larger research team. The primary responsibility will be to conduct statistical analyses, but the researcher will also provide consultation on analysis plans, prepare output tables, prepare analytic code for dissemination, and prepare analytic findings for presentation in reports to the State of Wisconsin and scholarly publications. The postdoctoral researcher will have the opportunity to initiate and lead analyses using the unique administrative data housed at UW IRP. The postdoc is planned for two years, conditional on satisfactory progress in year one, with a potential option to renew for a third year, contingent on performance and funding. A portion of the postdoc’s time will be reserved for independent research. Responsibilities: •\tRefine analytic plans for evaluating state and federal social welfare policies and programs. (10%) •\tPrepare and analyze complex research data, including linked administrative data from the Wisconsin Administrative Data Core, and prepare output. (55%) •\tConduct literature reviews and collaborate with the investigators to prepare reports, scholarly manuscripts, and dissemination materials based on research results. (20%) •\tConduct quality control reviews of code and organize and document code for dissemination as part of research transparency efforts. (10%) •\tCollaborate in developing grant applications and proposals to secure research funding for subsequent projects. (5%) Candidates will be evaluated in terms of research interests, scholarly potential, record of rigorous quantitative research, quality of writing, and strength of recommendations. Applications should be submitted by email to Jessica Pac, pac@wisc.edu. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue a rolling basis until the position is filled. Start date is flexible, between May and September 2027. Primary residence at UW-Madison is preferred, and some time in residence is required, but a majority virtual arrangement may be an option in exceptional circumstances. Selected applicants will receive an invitation to participate in virtual interviews. Those moving on to second round interviews will be invited to an in-person interview at UW-Madison. Once final applicants are identified, they will be asked to provide names and contact information for at least three professional references, including a current/most recent supervisor. At UW-Madison, we are dedicated to building a diverse, inclusive, and authentic workplace, so if you're excited about this role, we encourage you to apply. For questions on the position, contact: Jessica Pac pac@wisc.edu The ideal hiring salary for this position will be between $65,000-$75,000 for experienced candidates. Actual pay will depend on experience and qualifications. Employees in this position can expect to receive benefits such as generous vacation, holidays, and paid time off, competitive insurance and savings accounts, and retirement benefits. 100 05/01/2027 Jessica Pac pac@wisc.edu Approved 09/02/2026 6133 Postdoctoral Fellow (Postdoc) Embryo and Stem Cell Engineering Center for Biomedical Swine Research & Innovation Tanja Dominko, DVM, PhD 09/30/2026 Required Qualifications: Education: Ph.D. (or equivalent degree) in Molecular Biology, Mammalian Embryology, Biomedical Sciences, Cell Biology, Bioengineering, or a closely related field. Technical expertise: •\tDemonstrated hands-on experience with CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing and validation in mammalian cell systems. •\tExpertise in human and/or large animal (swine preferred) iPSC culture, reprograming, maintenance, and characterization. •\tExperience in swine embryology and assisted reproductive technologies (ART), including microinjection, micromanipulation, embryo handling, and in vitro culture. Preferred Qualifications: •\tExperience with Somatic Cell Nuclear Transfer (SCNT) in swine. •\tBackground in bioinformatics, transcriptomics, or computational analysis of NGS/RNA-seq and single-cell interactome data. •\tExperience with high-content imaging systems and flow cytometry/FACS sorting. •\tFamiliarity with immunology and xenotransplantation platforms. Required knowledge, skills, and abilities •\tStrong organizational skills and the ability to independently manage multi-step technical experimental timelines. •\tExcellent written and verbal communication skills for cross-functional collaboration with clinical, surgical, and industry research partners. •\tDemonstrated commitment to maintaining biosafety (BSL-2) and animal welfare standards. The Center for Biomedical Swine Research and Innovation (CBSRI) at the University of Wisconsin–Madison is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow to lead key research initiatives focused on interspecies blastocyst complementation to generate humanized vascular networks in swine organ models for xenotransplantation. The successful candidate will work under the direction of Dr. Tanja Dominko in collaboration with multidisciplinary teams across the UW–Madison School of Medicine. The primary responsibilities of this position involve CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing in porcine and human induced pluripotent stem cell (hiPSC) lines, functional validation of edits, and somatic cell nuclear transfer (SCNT). Key Responsibilities •\tGene editing and line engineering: Design, execute, and validate CRISPR/Cas9 target knockouts (e.g., bi-allelic ETV2 knockouts) and targeted promoter substitutions/knock-ins in both porcine and human iPSC lines. Perform long-read genomic DNA sequencing and molecular profiling for QC. •\tHuman and swine iPSC culture: Maintain, characterize, and optimize human and swine iPSC lines and conduct directed lineage differentiation assays •\tSwine embryology: Perform swine somatic cell nuclear transfer (SCNT) and assess survival, and preimplantation development in vitro. •\tData management & dissemination: Analyze computational and experimental datasets (including bioinformatics/transcriptomic interactomes), author manuscripts for high-impact peer-reviewed journals, and present research findings at national/international conferences. •\tRegulatory compliance: Maintain rigorous documentation in alignment with Institutional Biosafety Committee (IBC), SCRO, and IACUC guidelines. Interested candidates should submit the following application materials in a single PDF document to Dr. Tanja Dominko: 1.\tCover letter detailing your research interests, career goals, and how your technical expertise aligns with the project aims. 2.\tCurriculum Vitae including a full publication list. 3.\tContact information for three professional references. https://cbsri.wisc.edu/ 100 01/01/2027 Tanja Dominko dominko@wisc.edu Approved 09/01/2026 6128 Research Associate (Postdoc) Cell Biology BCM Feyza Engin 11/30/2026 Two Postdoctoral Research Associate Positions in Cellular Senescence and Immunology Engin Laboratory | University of Wisconsin–Madison The Engin Laboratory at the University of Wisconsin–Madison is seeking highly motivated scientists for two Postdoctoral Research Associate positions. One position will focus on cellular senescence and beta-cell biology, and the other on immunology and immune regulation in diabetes. Our research investigates the molecular and cellular mechanisms underlying beta-cell dysfunction and diabetes, with emphasis on the cellular stress, cellular senescence, and immune regulation. Our studies use human islet cultures, mouse models, cell-based systems, molecular and immunological approaches, flow cytometry, single-cell analysis, multi-omics, spatial transcriptomics, and bioinformatics. Representative publications include Engin et al., Science Translational Medicine (2013), Lee et al., Cell Metabolism (2020, 2023), Chen et al., Nature Communications (2022), and Lee et al., Nature Communications (2025). Recent Ph.D. graduates and candidates with prior postdoctoral experience (less than 3 years) are encouraged to apply. Position 1: Cell Biology and Cellular Senescence This position will investigate the role of cellular senescence in beta-cell dysfunction and diabetes. Qualifications A Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. in cell biology, biochemistry, molecular biology, genetics, or a related field is required. Required: •\tStrong research experience in cell biology and cellular senescence •\tExperience designing, performing, troubleshooting, and interpreting cell-based experiments •\tDemonstrated record of impactful peer-reviewed publications appropriate for career stage Highly desirable: •\tHuman islet culture and experimentation •\tMammalian cell culture •\tMolecular biology •\tRNA and protein analysis •\tSenescence assays •\tFluorescence or confocal imaging •\tMouse models of diabetes •\tSingle-cell RNA sequencing •\tMulti-omics •\tSpatial transcriptomics •\tBioinformatics and large-scale data analysis Position 2: Immunology and Diabetes This position will investigate the role of immune cells and inflammatory pathways in beta-cell dysfunction and diabetes. Qualifications A Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. in immunology, cell biology, biochemistry, molecular biology, genetics, or a related field is required. Required: •\tStrong research experience in immunology •\tExpertise in T-cell biology and/or macrophage biology •\tExperience designing, performing, troubleshooting, and interpreting immunological experiments •\tDemonstrated record of impactful peer-reviewed publications appropriate for career stage Highly desirable: •\tHuman islet culture and experimentation •\tT-cell activation, differentiation, and function •\tMacrophage biology and polarization •\tImmune-cell isolation and characterization •\tMulticolor flow cytometry (FACS) •\tCytokine and inflammatory signaling •\tImmune-cell/human islet co-culture •\tMammalian cell culture •\tMolecular biology •\tRNA and protein analysis •\tMouse models of diabetes, inflammation, or autoimmunity •\tSingle-cell RNA sequencing •\tMulti-omics •\tSpatial transcriptomics •\tBioinformatics and large-scale data analysis Experience with transgenic mouse colonies is advantageous but not required. Candidate Profile and Responsibilities We are seeking creative, productive, self-motivated scientists with strong experimental skills, critical-thinking abilities, and a commitment to collaborative research. The postdoctoral researchers will: •\tDesign and perform experiments and analyze data •\tDevelop novel research ideas •\tWork with human islets and relevant cellular and animal models •\tContribute to manuscripts and grant applications •\tMentor graduate and undergraduate students •\tPresent research at scientific meetings •\tDevelop an independent research program and pursue funding opportunities Application Applicants should submit a CV, cover letter describing their research experience and interests, and contact information for three references. Please email application materials to: Dr. Feyza Engin fengin@wisc.edu Please indicate in your cover letter whether you are applying primarily for the Cell Biology/Cellular Senescence position, the Immunology position, or both. For more information, please visit enginlab.org. nih based 100 FEYZA ENGIN fengin@wisc.edu (857) 413-9493 Approved 09/01/2026 6125 Postdoctoral Fellow (Postdoc) Cancer Biology Oncology Jing Zhang 12/31/2026 Candidates should have obtained their Ph.D and/or MD degree no more than 1 year. A strong foundation in cellular and molecular biology, and English proficiency are required, which should be demonstrated by publishing at least one peer-reviewed research article in an internationally recognized journal as the first author. Experience working with animals and flow cytometry is preferred. Postdoctoral fellow position is available immediately at Dr. Jing Zhang’s laboratory in the Department of Oncology at the University of Wisconsin-Madison for studying the molecular and cellular mechanisms underlying RAS-driven blood cancers, primarily focusing on acute myeloid leukemia. Current projects focus on multi-Omics studies, cancer immunology, developing and testing novel targeted therapies and immunotherapies in pre-clinical models. For full consideration, please submit your CV and a list of three references electronically to Dr. Jing Zhang at zhang@oncology.wisc.edu. https://mcardle.wisc.edu/faculty/jing-zhang/ NIH suggested salary scale 100 10/01/2026 Jing Zhang zhang@oncology.wisc.edu (608) 263-1147 Approved 08/31/2026 6119 Research Associate (Postdoc) Cell Biology and Immunology Medical Microbiology & Immunology Anna Huttenlocher 10/01/2026 A position is available for a Postdoctoral Scientist with experience in cell biology or immunology. The lab investigates the basic mechanisms that regulate cell migration and chemotaxis both in vitro and in vivo using zebrafish and human cells. One focus is on inflammation resolution signaling pathways. Applicants should submit a cover letter, C.V. and contact information for three references to Anna Huttenlocher. Please send all application materials by email (huttenlocher@wisc.edu). Anna Huttenlocher huttenlocher@wisc.edu Approved 08/28/2026 6116 Research Associate (Postdoc) Cancer biology, epigenetics, sarcoma Medicine Ryan Denu, MD, PhD 12/31/2026 - PhD in cancer biology, cell/molecular biology, epigenetics, computer science or computational biology - If experience in cell/molecular biology and/or epigenetics, the position would involve designing and carrying out research projects to contribute to the lab's mission of better understanding epigenetic and transcriptional regulation in sarcoma. This will involve culture of human and mouse cells, molecular biology experiments, epigenetic profiling, development of patient-derived xenografts and organoids, and use of immunodeficient and immunocompetent sarcoma mouse models. - If experience in computer science or computational biology, the position would involve designing and analyzing experiments using single cell and spatial analyses using primary human sarcoma specimens. It will also involve analyzing epigenetic datasets (e.g. CUT&RUN, ATAC-seq). Send a letter of interest, CV, and 2-3 references to Ryan Denu (rdenu@wisc.edu). https://www.medicine.wisc.edu/directory/denu_ryan Commensurate with experience using NIH Stipend Levels 100 Ryan Denu rdenu@wisc.edu (608) 890-0922 Approved 08/26/2026 6101 Research Associate (Postdoc) Normal hematopoiesis, Aging, Comparative Oncology Medical Sciences Dr. Xuan Pan 10/30/2026 The Pan laboratory is recruiting a highly motivated postdoc fellow with an interest in epigenetic regulations of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs). The vision for our research program is to apply a multidisciplinary approach and advance our innovative research program for understanding, and ultimately manipulating, HSC development, HSC cell fate and malignant transformation in hematopoiesis. These researches will support our long-term goal to advance fundamental knowledge and provide a foundation for novel diagnostic and therapeutic strategies for hematological cancers and other hematopoietic disorders. Candidates are required to have PhD, MD, DVM or equivalent doctoral degree. Candidates with extensive practical experience in bioinformatics, machine learning, molecular and cellular biology, and immunology with technique expertise in flow cytometry and mouse biology are encouraged to apply. Candidates must possess excellent organization skills and be capable of individual initiative and of interacting as part of team. Strong English speaking and writing skills are essential as well as a track record of peer reviewed publications. This is a fulltime appointment with complete coverage for medical insurance. Prospective applicants are encouraged to contact Xuan Pan, VMD/PhD, Professor, Medical Sciences Department, School of Veterinary Medicine, UW-Madison on 608-265-4718 or email: xpan24@wisc.edu with the current curriculum vitae and cover letter including names and contact information of three references. We look forward to a rewarding research experience for you. https://www.vetmed.wisc.edu/people/xpan24/ NIH standard Xuan Pan xpan24@wisc.edu (608) 265-4718 Approved 08/26/2026 6099 Research Intern Cancer biology, sarcoma, epigenetics Medicine Ryan Denu, MD, PhD 12/31/2026 •\tPrevious experience in a laboratory, preferably with prior experience with cell culture, molecular biology techniques (e.g. western blotting, DNA and RNA extraction, gene expression analysis, molecular cloning), and mouse handling. •\tConduct web lab experiments according to established protocols in the lab. These will mainly be involving human cell culture, molecular biology (e.g. cloning, PCR, etc), and mouse handling. •\tParticipate in the development, interpretation, and implementation of research methodology and materials. •\tSynthesize data from experiments, create figures, present data at lab meetings, and assist with preparation of manuscripts for publication. Send a letter of interest, CV, and 2-3 references to Ryan Denu (rdenu@wisc.edu). https://www.medicine.wisc.edu/directory/denu_ryan Minimum $30,952 to maximum $45,000 with benefits, based on education and experience 100 Ryan Denu rdenu@wisc.edu (608) 890-0922 Approved 08/26/2026 6098 Postdoctoral Fellow (Postdoc) Neurobiology of pain Anesthesiology Zachary T Campbell 10/01/2026 The Campbell lab (www.RNAcentral.com) is seeking a highly motivated post-doctoral scientist interested in understanding the molecular and cellular mechanisms of plasticity. We use genomic methodologies and molecular biology to probe how nascent translation is controlled in the context of pain. In specific, we seek candidates with the following qualifications: ·       Ph. D. in Neuroscience, Genetics, Physiology, Pharmacology, or related field ·       A minimum of two first author papers published or accepted in peer-reviewed journals ·       Strong background in behavior (experience with pain models is highly desirable) ·       Familiarity with animal surgery, mouse genotyping, confocal microscopy, western blots, and primary cell culture ·       Creative thinking skills and excellence in problem solving ·       Ability to work collaboratively ·       Excellent oral and written communication skills ·       Strong proficiency in the English language ·       Innate source of curiosity ·       Enthusiasm to learn new methods and test new hypotheses Opportunities: ·       Specialized training in genomics and molecular biology ·       Mentorship in proposal and manuscript generation ·       Next-generation sequencing and data interpretation ·       Ribosome profiling, SEQRS, and CLIP methodologies ·       Access to super-resolution imaging, 2 photon microscopy, spinning disc confocal, and cell sorting equipment ·       Collaborative work environment Interested candidates should send an email containing their CV and contact information for three references to zcampbell at wisc dot edu https://www.rnacentral.com/ The University of Wisconsin offers excellent benefits. Salary is based on research experience. The initial appointment is for 1 year with a possibility of extension. 100 09/29/2026 Zachary Campbell zcampbell@wisc.edu (608) 265-6710 Approved 08/24/2026 6092 Research Associate (Postdoc) Technology Entrepreneurship College of Engineering - Research Innovation John Krutty; William Murphy, Xiaolei Shi 09/11/2026 Qualifications: Key Skills: ●\tExtensive background and accomplishments in STEM research and technology development. ●\tKnowledge of industry relevant to a candidate's research expertise and experience with the economic, social, and environmental opportunities created by cross-disciplinary research. ●\tBusiness development, project management, and strategic partnership skills. Minimum number of years and type of relevant work experience: ●\tMinimum of three years’ experience in higher education, national laboratory, or academic STEM environment, with responsibilities involving research, technology development, product development, and project management, obtained through a Ph.D. in a STEM discipline or comparable project work. ●\tExcellent written and verbal communication skills required. ●\tExperience in a highly complex, matrixed organization such as a research center, a research-oriented university, or a large, commercial R&D organization is preferred. ●\tAbility to effectively evaluate new technologies and identify efficient routes toward broad impact on discovery. Education: ●\tRequired – PhD or equivalent terminal degree ●\tSTEM field preferred Description: Entrepreneurial Post Doc Innovation in highly technical fields mostly occurs within academia, yet academic innovators are generally unfamiliar with challenges related to transitioning basic discoveries into products at scale, or the commercial and regulatory oversight that many innovations face upon commercialization. These critical knowledge gaps may lead to uninformed decisions or inefficient approaches that threaten to derail development, significantly delay transition into the private sector, increase the risks and long-term costs of bringing such products to market, and decrease the value to potential partners. The Badger Tech Foundry has created a postdoctoral trainee program to address these knowledge gaps and facilitate the foundation of small business ventures. This program deals with cutting-edge technology across multiple verticals including biotechnology, medical devices, advanced materials, data science and AI, and clean energy applications. Role Overview: The Early Entrepreneur in Residence (EEIR) will be responsible for overseeing the pre-commercial development of novel innovations taking place at University of Wisconsin-Madison. The EEIR will collaborate with academic research teams, technical experts, external stakeholders, and actively manage the commercialization of products based on patented technology from researchers working with the Badger Tech Foundry. The EEIR will work with at least 1 up to a maximum of 3 technologies at various stages of development in which they will gain experience in the process of technology commercialization. This role is expected to select one of these projects for commercialization and drive toward incorporation and startup formation. In addition to hands-on work, this role will include a comprehensive educational program designed to develop the essential skills and knowledge necessary for effective technology entrepreneurship, commercialization, and early-stage product development. Ideal candidates will have recently finished a graduate program related to a STEM field, have experience in entrepreneurial ventures or education in technology commercialization, and knowledge of early-stage product development. The EEIR will gain hands-on experience with one of the technology verticals and approaching commercialization. Key Responsibilities: ●\tAssists with the identification of research problems and the development of research methodologies and procedures. ●\tCollects and analyzes research data, conducts experiments and interviews, and documents results according to established policies and procedures under general supervision. ●\tConducts literature reviews, prepares reports and materials, and disseminates information to appropriate entities. ●\tCollaborate with technical teams, external stakeholders, and Contract Development and Manufacturing Organizations (CDMOs) to develop technology across Regulatory, Technical, Business and Legal development milestones. ●\tEvaluate and record Technology Readiness Level (TRL) of each technology to prepare for small business spin-out. ●\tAssess and address translational regulatory challenges to facilitate the commercialization of technology. ●\tIdentify, evaluate, and assist in the application for funding related to small business creation, including SBIR/STTR and other sources of non-dilutive funding from government sources such as NIH, NSF, NASA, DoD and others. Additional Responsibilities: ●\tIdentify, create, and/or manage collaborative efforts with student, academic, and professional groups ●\tSupport development of commercialization-related materials including pitch decks, executive summaries, funding proposals, and business plans ●\tParticipate in strategic planning initiatives and interact with the Program Director to develop policies and procedures, evaluate operational needs, strategy, and research developments To apply, please send a CV and cover letter to badgertechfoundry@engr.wisc.edu with the subject line EEIR Application Standard postdoc scale 100 John Krutty krutty@wisc.edu Approved 08/21/2026 6085 Research Intern Children's emotional and cognitive development Psychology Professor Seth Pollak 10/01/2026 Bachelor’s or Master’s in psychology, social work, education, or a related field. A Research Intern position is available at the Child Emotion Research Laboratory at the Waisman Center. The laboratory studies the development of emotions and stress-related difficulties in children ages 4-12. The Research Intern will assist with experiments to support forthcoming research publications, as well as assist with outreach efforts to families in the community. The position requires a minimum of 2 years of direct experience working with children and families. In addition, applicants must have some awareness of social and community service organizations in the greater Madison area that serve children and families. Position can be 50% - 100% time. Send a letter of interest, CV, and three references to Anna Bechner (ambechner@wisc.edu). https://childemotion.waisman.wisc.edu Minimum $30,952 to maximum $45,000 with benefits, based on education and experience 100 09/01/2026 Anna Bechner ambechner@wisc.edu (608) 890-2525 Approved 08/21/2026 6084 Research Associate (Postdoc) Bioinformatics, Microbiome Science, Plant Pathology Plant Pathology Jonathan Jacobs 09/30/2026 Required Qualifications •\tPh.D. in bioinformatics, computational biology, genomics, microbiology, plant pathology, or a related field (or expected to be completed before the start date). •\tDemonstrated experience in bioinformatic analysis of genomic and/or metagenomic sequence data. •\tProficiency in at least one scripting/programming language commonly used in bioinformatics (e.g., Python, R). •\tStrong written and oral communication skills, including a record of scientific writing and presentation. •\tWillingness to complete required biosecurity and biosafety training and to be approved for access to Select Agent pathogen data and, as applicable, Select Agent laboratory spaces (background screening and program registration required). Desired Qualifications •\tExperience applying generative AI or machine learning approaches to biological data. •\tExperience with high-performance computing environments and reproducible workflow/pipeline development. •\tExperience mentoring or supervising student researchers. The Jacobs Laboratory in the Department of Plant Pathology at the University of Wisconsin–Madison seeks a Postdoctoral Research Associate with strong computational skills to lead bioinformatic analyses supporting research on pathogen evolution, host–microbe interactions, and microbiome science. The lab uses comparative and functional genomics, metagenomics, and generative AI/machine learning approaches to understand how microbial pathogens and microbiome communities evolve, adapt, and interact with their hosts. This position is well suited to a computational biologist who wants to apply cutting-edge bioinformatic and AI methods to questions in microbiology, pathogen biology, and microbiome ecology. This role will also involve computational work with biosecurity-sensitive datasets, including genomic and metagenomic sequence data derived from USDA/CDC Select Agent pathogens, requiring strict adherence to institutional data security and biosecurity compliance requirements. Research Foundation This position draws on a rich foundation of genomic and evolutionary research on pathogenic bacteria and microbiomes. Our team searches for an individual to advance our efforts around microbial lineage emergence. We have evolutionary genomics approaches to define mechanisms of bacterial evolution and niche-specificity (Gluck-Thaler et al. 2020, Science Advances; Verma et al. 2024 PLoS Pathogens; Peduzzi et al. 2025 Nature Ecology & Evolution; Verma et al. 2026 ISME Journal; Merfa et al. 2026 PLoS Pathogens). Complementary work on microbial lineage tracking and emergence has helped us with pathogen tracking related to agriculture (Roman-Reyna et al. 2021 mSystems; Roman-Reyna et al. 2024; Toth et al. bioRxiv). This postdoc will apply and advance these computational frameworks to new datasets, develop AI/ML approaches for integrating data, and contribute novel insights into the molecular basis of bacterial evolution and adaptation. Application To apply, please submit a cover letter describing research interests and career goals, a curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. https://plantpath.wisc.edu/faculty/jacobs-jonathan/ 10/01/2026 Jonathan Jacobs jonathan.jacobs@wisc.edu Approved 08/11/2026 6046 Research Associate (Postdoc) Stress fracture injury prevention screening in Thoroughbred Racehorses School of Veterinary Medicine, Department of Surgical Sciences Peter Muir, Corinne Henak 10/15/2026 A candidate with a PhD in mechanical engineering, biomedical engineering or a similar field, with experience in finite element analysis, AI computer vision analysis of medical images, and mechanical testing is preferred. The candidate will be expected to develop expertise in finite element analysis and AI deep learning models. A postdoctoral position in mechanical testing and finite element modeling is available in the Comparative Orthopaedic Research Laboratory (School of Veterinary Medicine) and the Henak Laboratory (College of Engineering) at UW-Madison. The long-term goal of this research project is to reduce serious injuries in Thoroughbred racehorses, many of which result in euthanasia, by leveraging the recent development of standing computed tomography (sCT) for horses via Asto CT. The short-range goals, which the postdoc will accomplish with the research team, are to continue to develop virtual mechanical testing pipelines using 3D finite element analysis, together with AI automation of CT image segmentation, to develop analysis pipelines suitable for clinical implementation in injury prevention screening of Thoroughbred racehorses. In this sCT screening, the goal is to identify horses with imminent elevated risk of fetlock stress fracture, a particularly common cause of fatal injury. The engineer will work with other team members to analyze and report results. The candidate will be co-supervised by a veterinary clinician-scientist and an engineer, and will form part of an interdisciplinary team. The project is funded through the Grayson-Jockey Club Research Foundation. Applications for the position should include a CV, cover letter stating research background and interests, and contact information for three professional references.  Please send applications to: Corinne Henak, PhD Associate Professor Department of Mechanical Engineering chenak@wisc.edu Peter Muir, BVSc, PhD, DACVS, FRCVS Professor Department of Surgical Sciences peter.muir@wisc.edu 63,480 100 11/01/2026 Peter Muir peter.muir@wisc.edu Approved 08/07/2026 6032 Research Associate (Postdoc) Biochemistry and Biophysics (eEF2K Signaling and Translational Control) Anesthesiology Sarah Loerch 09/15/2026 The Loerch Laboratory at UW–Madison is seeking a biochemist or molecular/cell biologist to investigate how eukaryotic elongation factor 2 kinase (eEF2K) is regulated and how it controls translation in neuronal signaling. Applicants must have: A PhD in biochemistry, molecular biology, cell biology, neuroscience, or a related field by the start date. Experience studying protein kinases, cell-signaling pathways, or related regulatory mechanisms. Strong training in biochemical and cell-based experimental approaches. Experience independently designing, executing, and interpreting experiments. A record of research productivity and strong communication skills. Experience with neuronal systems, quantitative kinase assays, protein–protein interactions, or translational control is desirable. Structural biology experience is welcome but not required. The Loerch Laboratory is seeking a biochemist or molecular/cell biologist to investigate the regulation and biological functions of eukaryotic elongation factor 2 kinase (eEF2K). The project will examine how signaling pathways regulate eEF2K activity and how eEF2K-dependent translational control shapes neuronal responses and pain-associated plasticity. The successful candidate will use biochemical and cell-based approaches to study kinase signaling, protein interactions, and translational regulation, with opportunities to integrate biochemical, biophysical, structural, protein engineering and cell-based approaches. Responsibilities include designing and conducting experiments, analyzing and presenting results, preparing manuscripts, participating in collaborative projects, and mentoring junior researchers. Please email the following materials to Dr. Sarah Loerch at loerch2@wisc.edu: Cover letter describing your relevant research experience and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae. Two or three relevant publications. Names, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. Use the subject line “eEF2K Postdoctoral Application.” Applications will be reviewed as received until the position is filled. https://loerchlab.com/ NIH scale 100 Sarah Loerch loerch2@wisc.edu Approved 07/30/2026 6002 Research Associate (Postdoc) High-speed flows, multiphase flows, fluid mixing and reaction Mechanical Engineering Jacob McFarland 10/31/2026 •\tPh.D. in Engineering, or a closely related field (required) •\tProven experience with experimental methods in fluid flows, ideally high-speed and/or multiphase flows •\tStrong publication record in relevant scientific journals •\tExperience mentoring students and working in collaborative research environments •\tExcellent communication skills •\tPerform experimental studies in high-speed flow environments, including setup, data acquisition, and analysis •\tDevelop and implement innovative models and methods for fluid mixing and droplet breakup •\tDisseminate research outcomes through peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations •\tMentor graduate and undergraduate students in experimental techniques and research methodology •\tCollaborate with WiSTL faculty, and pursue external research funding opportunities Interested candidates should submit a CV, cover letter detailing research experience and interests, and contact information for at least two references to Professor Jacob McFarland at mcfarland4@wisc.edu 75000 100 02/01/2027 Jacob McFarland mcfarland4@wisc.edu Approved 07/30/2026 6001 Research Intern Cell Biology Pharmaceutical Sciences Division Arash Bashirullah 09/30/2026 Bachelor’s or Master’s in Cell Biology, Genetics, Molecular Biology, or a related field A Research Intern position is available in Dr. Arash Bashirullah’s lab to study the role of bridge-like lipid transfer proteins (BLTPs) in human disease. The BLTPs are an emerging family of intracellular lipid transporters that move lipids in bulk between donor and recipient membranes at organelle membrane contact sites. Mutations in BLTPs are associated with neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative diseases in humans. The Bashirullah Lab is particularly focused on studying the cellular, developmental, and physiological roles of BLTP2, using Drosophila as a model system. The Research Intern will assist with experiments to support forthcoming research publications, working closely with Dr. Sarah Neuman, an experienced staff scientist in the lab. Send a letter of interest, CV, and three references to Sarah Neuman (sdneuman@wisc.edu). Minimum $30,952 to maximum $45,000 with benefits, based on education and experience 100 Sarah Neuman sdneuman@wisc.edu Approved 07/29/2026 5994 Research Associate (Postdoc) Synthetic and Medicinal Chemistry Pharmaceutical Sciences Dr. Jennifer E. Golden 11/01/2026 The laboratory of Dr. Jennifer E. Golden at the University of Wisconsin-Madison (UW-Madison) is seeking two (2) Postdoctoral Research Associates. Successful candidates will join a highly collaborative, multidisciplinary and multi-institutional team working to develop an antiviral small molecule for pre-IND (investigational new drug) FDA review. REQUIREMENTS include: • A PhD in synthetic organic chemistry or strong synthetic chemistry focused, related discipline • Excellent experimental and laboratory record keeping • Excellent communication and interpersonal skills in English • Meet project deadlines through effective planning, proactive communication of risks or delays, and timely implementation of corrective actions • Ensure strict adherence to laboratory chemical hygiene plans, safety protocols, and institutional policies, maintaining a safe and compliant work environment in the laboratory and surrounding facilities. Preferred Qualifications include • Experience with scaling known organic chemistry reactions from gram to multigram scale • At least one first-author research publication in a peer-reviewed, internationally recognized journal. • Ability to work independently, cooperatively, and thrive in a collaborative, interdisciplinary environment. Each researcher will be responsible for the execution of an iterative, multistep synthesis on multigram scale (5-50 g). Thorough analysis of chemical products by standard methods (NMR, MS, trace metal analysis, chiral HPLC analysis for enantiomeric purity) is expected, along with tracking of experimental details, purity, product characterization, and cost. The goal is to deliver a high quality research grade chemical product for use by the extended team at other research organizations for in vivo studies that establish safety and efficacy of a potential drug product. This position offers access to state-of-the-art facilities and collaborations with experts in virology, pharmacology, medicinal and process chemistry. The role is ideal for researchers seeking exposure to a drug discovery platform that started from screening hits and has advanced to a preclinical asset with experience offered in late stage synthetic efforts and engagement with models used to evaluate efficacy and toxicity. To apply, email the following materials to Dr. J. E. Golden at jennifer.golden@wisc.edu • CV • Cover letter summarizing research experience and interests • Contact information for three references 08/01/2026 Jennifer Golden jennifer.golden@wisc.edu Approved 07/28/2026 5992 Research Associate (Postdoc) Alzheimer's Prevention Department of Neurology Dr. Jessica Caldwell 08/11/2026 PhD in Psychology, Clinical Psychology, Cognitive Neuroscience or related field. Research experience required in dementia, biomarkers, cognitive assessment, and/or longitudinal data analysis. Competitive candidates will have a strong track record of publishing in these areas. Research within the Wisconsin Registry for Alzheimer’s Prevention and other studies run by Dr. Jessica Caldwell. Access to sensitive populations is to research participant data including PHI, though contact face to face is possible at study visits or research outreach events. Please submit a CV and cover letter detailing your experience and interest to Dr. Jessica Caldwell (caldwell@neurology.wisc.edu). NIH Rate 100 10/01/2026 Jessica Caldwell caldwell@neurology.wisc.edu Expired 07/10/2026 5970 Postgraduate Trainee Research related to understanding the presentation, treatment, and mechanisms relevant to psychopathology or stress-related disorders Psychiatry Ned Kalin, MD 09/30/2026 PhD and or MD, DO  degrees  with research focus in  the presentation, treatment, and mechanisms relevant to psychopathology and other stress-related disorders. Work in an established laboratory to participate in and lead research initiatives relevant to providing a deeper understanding of psychopathology and its treatment.  Work could be performed with clinical interviews, collection of biomarkers,  neuroimaging, and wet lab based research including molecular studies, sleep studies, and nonhuman primate animal models of anxiety and studies focusing on mechanisms of psychedelic drug actions. Please send a Cover Letter and CV to KaBao Chang at kchang52@wisc.edu. NIH Stipend Salary 100 01/01/2027 KaBao Chang kchang52@wisc.edu Approved 07/08/2026 5963 Postdoctoral Fellow (Postdoc) Cognitive Psychology Department of Psychology C. Shawn Green 11/01/2026 Successful candidates should have strong technical skills, including in data analysis (R preferred), management skills, creativity and a high-level of conscientiousness, as these are required to play a lead role in this sizeable project. They should also have expertise in one or more of the following areas (and knowledge of the others): working memory, cognitive training, perceptual learning, and methods of assessing individual differences and effects of interventional studies. Experience in programming psychophysical experiments, in either MATLAB or Python, is preferred. Researchers must possess a doctoral degree in Psychology, Education, Cognitive Science, Neuroscience, or a related field. The ideal starting time for the position would be during the Fall of 2026. However, we will also consider alternative timelines for the right candidate. Thus, individuals planning to complete their dissertation during Summer/Fall 2026 are encouraged to apply. Review of materials will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. C. Shawn Green (UW-Madison), in partnership with Aaron Seitz and Susanne Jaeggi (Northeastern University, Boston), is seeking to hire a post-doctoral researcher to work on a project funded by the National Institute on Aging. The project is focused on investigating features of cognitive training that differentially lead to learning and transfer of learning in both young adults (college-aged individuals) as well as in older adults. The post-doctoral researcher is expected to take a lead role in the project, including setting up experiments, collecting and analyzing data, training and supervision of graduate students and undergraduate research assistants, and dissemination of results at conferences and in research publications. The primary appointment will be at the University of Wisconsin-Madison in the Learning and Transfer Lab of C. Shawn Green (https://greenlab.psych.wisc.edu/). The lab’s broad focus is to examine factors that alter the rate and generality of learning in both the perceptual and cognitive domains. The University of Wisconsin offers excellent benefits. Salary is based on research experience. The initial appointment is for 1 year with a possibility of extension. Furthermore, although the postdoc’s primary location will be at UW-Madison, the postdoc will work closely with both Seitz and Jaeggi to ensure that procedures are coordinated between sites and that all personnel are properly trained and supervised. Candidates should send a cover letter, CV, pdfs of 2-3 relevant publications, and the names of three references with phone numbers and email addresses to: C. Shawn Green (cshawn.green@wisc.edu). https://greenlab.psych.wisc.edu/ Based on Research Experience 100 09/01/2026 C. Shawn Green cshawn.green@wisc.edu (608) 262-1040 Approved 07/07/2026 5960 Research Intern Plant Genetics and Plant Breeding Plant and Agroecosystem Sciences Jean-Michel Ané 08/16/2026 A BS or MS in Plant Genetics, Plant Breeding, Agronomy, Horticulture, or related fields A Research Intern position is available in Dr. Jean-Michel Ané’s laboratory (http://anelab.wisc.edu/) in the departments of Bacteriology and Plant and Agroecosystem Sciences at the University of Wisconsin - Madison. The Research Intern will be part of an interdisciplinary team studying the genetic basis of nitrogen fixation in the mucilage produced by the aerial roots of maize and sorghum. The Research Intern will characterize plant genes controlling the trait using genetics and genomics. The Research Intern will also be involved in breeding approaches. This project is a close collaboration with Dr. Natalia de Leon at the University of Wisconsin – Madison. For more information on the scientific background, please read: https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.3003037 https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.2006352 https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1360138519303292 Please email a cover letter, a full CV, and 3 references to Dr. Jean-Michel Ané (jeanmichel.ane@wisc.edu) and entitle your email “Application for Cereals Genetics and Breeding Intern Position”. https://anelab.wisc.edu/ $38K/year + full benefits 100 Jean-Michel Ane jeanmichel.ane@wisc.edu Expired 07/06/2026 5949 Research Associate (Postdoc) Computational Neuroscience, Philosophy, Computer Science Psychiatry Giulio Tononi, MD, PhD 12/31/2026 -PhD in Computational neuroscience, Computer science, Philosophy or areas related to Consciousness science -Preferred skills: Python programming, familiarity with neural networks and agentic AI, and/or academic philosophy writing. -Expertise: Familiarity with consciousness science methods and