some flexibility for the Fellow to pursue projects and opportunities that align with their interests and provide benefits to the Wisconsin Coastal Management Program and Wisconsin Sea Grant. Some specific opportunities include: ●\tHelp finalize updates to and provide guided outreach on Wisconsin Sea Grant’s Coastal Processes Manual (3rd Ed). This manual demonstrates methods to assess the threat of hazards like flooding, shoreline erosion, and dangerous currents and waves to coastal properties and communities. Efforts may include assisting with editing and production, coordinating outreach events and trainings, developing tools or hazard data to support use of the manual, and piloting specific chapters and tools in communities. ●\tSupport a mapping effort to update the Wisconsin Shoreline Inventory and Oblique Viewer. This interactive map provides coastal communities access to geospatial data at a resolution that can support local decision-making. Efforts may include documenting a methodology to update shoreline condition information, updating data layers, interpreting shoreline condition trends, surveying end-users and developing recommendations for future platform updates, and developing outreach materials to help decision-makers utilize the available data. ●\tProvide support and participate in activities of the Wisconsin Coastal Management Program including attending Wisconsin Coastal Management Council meetings, assisting with annual grants program, updating the grants program StoryMap, and facilitating workshops and trainings, and participating in coastal resilience working groups. ●\tProvide support and participate in activities of the Wisconsin Sea Grant Program by attending team meetings, assisting with research, fellowship, internship, or outreach projects and supporting the biennial competitive grant review process. ●\tEngage with and support existing networks of local, state, federal and university partners that are working collaboratively on coastal resilience including Collaborative Action for Lake Michigan Resilience (CALM) and Coastal Hazards of Lake Superior (CHAOS). ●\tExplore opportunities to collaborate with Fellows and professionals in other state agencies (e.g., Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources) on topics such as flooding, wetlands, habitat, and coastal resilience. ●\tCommunicate coastal resilience science and policy work at state, regional, and national conferences. Application packages should be sent to jennifer.hauxwell@aqua.wisc.edu at the University of Wisconsin Sea Grant Institute and should include: 1. A personal statement that describes your background, your expectations from the fellowship experience and how this experience fits with your career goals (2 pages or fewer) 2. Curriculum vitae with relevant educational, professional and volunteer experience (no length limit) 3. Copies of all undergraduate and graduate student transcripts (unofficial copies ok) 4. Up to 4 writing samples, both formal and informal (e.g., journal articles or other technical documents, popular articles, web resources, etc.) 5. A list of two professional references with contact information, including a faculty member from your graduating institution familiar with your academic record Please use the naming convention “Last name – description of file” for all files associated with the application (e.g. “Yang – cover letter”, “Yang – cv”, “Yang – transcripts”, etc.). https://uwmadison.app.box.com/s/4r648uw3r441d01bxmfqn8ftq78q33aj Annual stipends are dependent on the Fellow’s academic background, with post master’s Fellows earning $45,000 and postdoctoral Fellows earning $59,000, both with additional benefits (see - http://www.ohr.wisc.edu/benefits/new-emp/grad.aspx). Fellows will be allotted up to $2,000 to cover fellowship-related travel and can include conferences. Additional travel associated with the fellowship may be covered by the host agency at the agency’s discretion. 100 Jennifer Hauxwell jennifer.hauxwell@aqua.wisc.edu Expired 07/02/2026 5916 Research Intern Immunology and Inflammation Medical Microbiology & Immunology Anna Huttenlocher 08/01/2026 - Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field - One year of research experience in a laboratory setting is preferred; relevant undergraduate research experience will be considered. - Experience with molecular biology and microscopy preferred Cell biology research team seeks organized, self-driven individual for a research internship focused on innate immunology. The Huttenlocher lab studies cell migration in normal and pathological processes such as wound healing, tumor metastasis and chronic inflammation using human cell culture and zebrafish as models.  The research internship will utilize transgenic and mutant zebrafish lines to study innate immunology using microscopy and molecular biology.  The position is guaranteed for 1 year with the possibility of a one-year extension dependent on funding. Please respond by e-mail with statement of interest and CV to julie.rindy@wisc.edu. https://huttenlocher.labs.wisc.edu/ Dependent on experience 100 09/01/2026 Anna Huttenlocher huttenlocher@wisc.edu Expired 06/30/2026 5907 Research Associate (Postdoc) Comparative Genetics & Orthopaedic Research Laboratory School of Veterinary Medicine Peter Muir and Susannah Sample 08/15/2026 A candidate with a PhD in quantitative genetics in animal science or a similar field with experience in Bayesian regression and analysis of SNP marker data is preferred. The candidate will be expected to further develop expertise in Bayesian probit model regression and will work with other team members to analyze and report results. The candidate will be co-supervised by two veterinary clinician-scientists. The laboratory has close collaborative links with the other quantitative genetics investigators at UW-Madison. The project is funded through the Companion Animal Fund of the School of Veterinary Medicine, the American Rottweiler Club, and donations to our laboratory. Multi-ancestry prediction of cruciate ligament rupture using a novel Bayesian probit mixture model: A postdoctoral position in complex trait quantitative genetics is available in the Comparative Genetics & Orthopaedic Research Laboratory (School of Veterinary Medicine) at UW-Madison. The long-term goal of this research project is to develop robust methods of multi-ancestry polygenic risk score prediction of common diseases on companion animals, principally dogs and horses. We are also interested in studying shared genetic variation in specific orthopaedic diseases, such as anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) rupture, between dogs and humans. Additionally, we are interested in computer vision AI automation of diagnostic imaging in veterinary medicine, principally abdominal digital radiography in dogs and fetlock standing computed tomography in racing Thoroughbred horses. The short-term goals, which the postdoc will accomplish with the research team, are to continue development of a novel Bayesian probit mixture model for joint analysis of ACL rupture in different breeds of dog, extending current work on multi-ancestry prediction using machine learning, Bayesian regression, and deep learning methods for polygenic risk score prediction. Applications for the position should include a CV, cover letter stating research background and interests, and contact information for three professional references. Please send applications to: Peter Muir BVSc, PhD, DACVS, FRCVS Professor Department of Surgical Sciences peter.muir@wisc.edu Susannah J. Sample DVM, MS, PhD, DACVS Associate Professor Department of Surgical Sciences susannah.sample@wisc.edu https://www.vetmed.wisc.edu/lab/corl/ $63,480 100 09/01/2026 Laura Hess LHess4@wisc.edu Expired 06/22/2026 5881 Research Associate (Postdoc) Microbiology or Chemistry Food Science Tu-Anh Huynh 09/17/2026 Required qualifications: • PhD in Microbiology, Chemistry, Biochemistry, or a related field. • Strong record of productivity and ownership of research projects. • Excellent organization and communication skills. Preferred qualifications (one of the followings): • Experience with bacterial genetics and molecular cloning • Experience with protein chemistry and analytical chemistry • Experience with omics experimental techniques and data analysis, such as RNAseq, proteomics, or 16S sequencing •\tDesign and perform experiments related to bacterial genetics, protein chemistry, bacterial pathogenesis, or antimicrobial discovery •\tAnalyze and present data at group meetings and prepare manuscripts for publication •\tSupport other group members with experimental efforts or daily operations in the lab Interested applicants should email the following application materials, combined in one file, to Dr. Tu-Anh Huynh (thuynh6@wisc.edu): i) A one-page summary of research experience, career goals, and a potential project that aligns with our current research interests  interests ii) A CV including 2-3 contacts for references https://huynhlab.labs.wisc.edu/ $52,000 minimum 100 10/01/2026 Tu-Anh Huynh thuynh6@wisc.edu Approved 06/17/2026 5868 Research Intern Molecular Biology Pathobiological Sciences Mostafa Zamanian 07/18/2026 Required Qualifications: - At least 2 years of experience in a biomedical research setting - Experience working both collaboratively and independently - Demonstrated experience with accurate record keeping and compliance - Bachelor's Degree required by start date; focus in a biomedical or animal-related field preferred Preferred Qualifications: - Experience in molecular biology techniques. - Comfortable working with insects and rodents. Job Summary: The overall responsibility of this position will be to assist researchers with laboratory experiments, maintain biological colonies, and support day-to-day laboratory and research operations under moderate supervision. This role involves contributing to ongoing research projects through data collection, record keeping, and adherence to established protocols, while ensuring a well-organized and efficient laboratory environment. Excellent communication skills and the ability to work both collaboratively and independently are essential. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail are essential for success in this position. This position may require some work to be performed in-person, onsite, at a designated campus work location. May include some weekends. Candidates should demonstrate an ability to multitask and pay attention to detail, as well as strong communication skills Key Job Responsibilities: - Mosquito husbandry and insectary upkeep. Oversee undergrads carrying out insectary tasks. (30%) - Management of parasite infections in mosquito and rodent models. (15%) - General lab upkeep and maintenance. Operates, cleans, and maintains lab equipment according to lab guidelines. (15%) - Conducts research experiments according to established research protocols. Collects data and maintains well-organized lab records. (40%) How to apply: Cover letter required Resume Kathy Vacarro kvaccaro@wisc.edu Expired 06/17/2026 5867 Research Associate (Postdoc) Acute brain injury; Stroke Neurological Surgery Suresh Mehta, PhD 09/09/2026 The Mehta Lab, in close collaboration with the Vemuganti Group, is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Researcher to join our vibrant, team-oriented research environment. Our program focuses on uncovering noncoding RNA crosstalk in driving secondary brain damage following stroke and traumatic brain injury (TBI). Specifically, we investigate how various noncoding RNAs (including microRNAs, lncRNAs, and circRNAs) influence neuronal injury and subsequent recovery. Ideal candidates should possess the following: -In Vivo Experience: Working with rodent models of stroke and/or TBI, including behavioral analysis. -Molecular & Cellular Biology Skills: Proficiency in standard laboratory techniques, including histopathology and immunohistochemistry (IHC), Western blotting and PCR, RNA and protein profiling, and siRNA-mediated gene modulation -Communication: Excellent written and verbal English communication skills. -Core Values: A strong commitment to research ethics, scientific integrity, and a collaborative team mindset. -Preferred Skills: Advanced data analysis and bioinformatics skills and a strong track record of scientific writing and publications -Education: A PhD in neuroscience, biomedical sciences, or a closely related field. This role requires close interaction and teamwork with other scientists, postdoctoral fellows, and graduate students in our dynamic, collaborative research environment. -Conduct cutting-edge translational neuroscience research in a highly collaborative setting. -Lead independent projects while mentoring graduate students and junior lab members. -Analyze complex datasets and contribute significantly to scientific manuscript preparation and grant writing. If you are passionate about cutting-edge neuroscience research and eager to contribute to discoveries that may improve outcomes for patients with brain injuries, we encourage you to apply. If you are interested, please submit a CV and a brief cover letter that includes your prior research experience, career goals, and the names of two referees to mehta@neurosurgery.wisc.edu. https://www.neurosurgery.wisc.edu/staff/vemuganti-raghu/ NIH Stipend Level 100 Suresh Mehta mehta@neurosurgery.wisc.edu Approved 06/09/2026 5846 Research Intern Neurodegeneration, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis Human Oncology Randal Tibbetts 07/31/2026 Bachelors degree (certificates in genetics, biochemistry, molecular biology, neuroscience, chemistry, or related disciplines preferred). One year of undergraduate lab experience and experience with bioinformatic approaches (R, Python, Bioconductor, etc) also preferred but not essential. Perform mentored research related to the molecular pathogenesis of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and frontotemporal dementia (FTD). Duties  include: (i) 